#!/bin/sh
# demo-block-check — `deploy/demo.md`'s yaml block must still be `.github/workflows/demo-deploy.yml`.
#
# THE DUPLICATION IS DELIBERATE AND STAYS (quince#622). `deploy/demo.md` carries a byte-identical
# copy of the deploy workflow, and its own prose says why: *"It lives here rather than in a PR
# comment so there is one copy rather than two that drift."*
#
# ITS ORIGINAL REASON HAS EXPIRED, AND THE DUPLICATION SURVIVES IT. This read *"the reason is
# structural — no agent seat can write under `.github/workflows/**` (quince#113, measured for the
# implementer PAT, the architect PAT and the coder App) — so the reference copy has to live
# somewhere an agent can actually maintain."* That is **false since 2026-08-17**: `quince-coder`
# was granted `workflows: write` and pushed one (quince#1116), as `quince-review[bot]` already
# could (quince#894). Corrected rather than deleted, because the sentence is what the refusal at
# the bottom of this file was routing on, and a reader who fixes one without the other keeps the
# misrouting.
#
# What the duplication is worth NOW is the near-miss below: a reviewable copy in a document, which
# is what caught the one real defect this arrangement has seen. Deleting it is still not the fix.
#
# THE NEAR-MISS, which is the whole argument. quince#618 installed `cron: "0 22 * * *"` — a value
# `deploy/demo.md` had already tried and moved away from, quoting GitHub that top-of-hour queued
# jobs *"may be dropped"*. Two seats found it independently by READING, minutes apart, and it was
# corrected before merge. That is quince#408's argument arriving again: care worked, twice, and
# care is not a control.
#
# WHO PAYS WHEN IT ROTS is what makes this worth a gate rather than attention. If the workflow is
# the side that is wrong, no agent seat can repair it and the Operator must be interrupted. A
# control that turns *"the Operator must be interrupted"* from a discovery into a gate failure is
# worth more here than the same control over two files an implementer owns.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SYMMETRIC ON PURPOSE: IT NAMES BOTH PATHS AND PICKS NO WINNER.
#
# The gate can say *they differ*. It cannot say *which is right*, and it must not try. quince#618 is
# the evidence: the doc was the correct side and the workflow was the drifted one, so a gate that
# resolved toward the workflow — the obvious choice, since the workflow is what actually runs —
# would have picked wrong on the only instance there has ever been.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ONE PAIR, HARDCODED. Architect ruling on quince#622, and it is a ruling against generality: a
# reusable *"this fence mirrors that file"* mechanism needs a marker convention in the prose, which
# every future document author would then have to know. `bin/gap-heading-check` declined to pay that
# and so does this. A second pair is when to revisit, not before.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY *EXACTLY ONE* YAML FENCE, RATHER THAN "THE FIRST ONE".
#
# quince#622 and its ruling both propose `awk '/^```yaml$/{f=1;next} /^```$/{if(f)exit} f'`, which
# takes the FIRST yaml fence in the file. That is right today — `deploy/demo.md` has exactly one —
# and it fails SILENTLY the day somebody adds a second yaml example above it: the gate then compares
# the wrong block against the workflow, and reports a difference that is about neither file's
# correctness. A green gate that is answering a different question is the failure this repository
# keeps filing, so the count is asserted instead of assumed.
#
# Zero fences and two fences are both DID NOT RUN rather than a finding, because in both the
# comparison never happened. The refusal names the count and the file, so the remedy is visible from
# the message: either restore the block, or teach this gate which fence is the mirror.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Three exit codes, the contract every gate in this repository uses (quince#41):
#
#   0  the block and the workflow are byte-identical
#   1  they differ — the diff is printed, both paths are named, neither is declared authoritative
#   2  DID NOT RUN — a file is missing, or the fence is absent, doubled, unterminated or empty
#
# A 2 is NEVER clean. It is the case where a silent pass would be worst: a missing or mangled fence
# is exactly the state a careless edit to `deploy/demo.md` leaves behind, and it is indistinguishable
# from "the two agree" to anything that only reads an exit code.
set -eu

ME=$(basename "$0")

DOC_DEFAULT=deploy/demo.md
WORKFLOW_DEFAULT=.github/workflows/demo-deploy.yml

die2() { printf '%s: DID NOT RUN — %s\n' "$ME" "$1" >&2; exit 2; }

usage() {
	cat >&2 <<EOF
usage: $ME [<doc> <workflow>]

  With no arguments, checks the one pair this repository has:
      $DOC_DEFAULT   (its single \`\`\`yaml fence)
      $WORKFLOW_DEFAULT

  Both paths may be given explicitly; that exists for the suite, which builds fixtures
  in a temp dir. Passing one without the other is an error, not a default.

exit: 0 identical · 1 they differ · 2 DID NOT RUN
EOF
}

for a in "$@"; do
	case $a in
	-h | --help)
		usage
		exit 0
		;;
	-*) die2 "unknown option $a — see --help" ;;
	esac
done

case "$#" in
0)
	DOC=$DOC_DEFAULT
	WORKFLOW=$WORKFLOW_DEFAULT
	;;
2)
	DOC=$1
	WORKFLOW=$2
	;;
*) die2 "expected no arguments or exactly two (<doc> <workflow>), got $# — see --help" ;;
esac

[ -f "$DOC" ] || die2 "$DOC does not exist"
[ -f "$WORKFLOW" ] || die2 "$WORKFLOW does not exist"

# The fence opener, counted rather than assumed — see the header. `grep -c` exits 1 on no match,
# which `set -e` would take as a script failure, so the count is captured with the exit swallowed.
opens=$(grep -n '^```yaml$' "$DOC" | cut -d: -f1) || opens=""
count=$(printf '%s' "$opens" | grep -c . ) || count=0

case "$count" in
0) die2 "$DOC has no \`\`\`yaml fence — the mirrored block is gone, so there was nothing to compare" ;;
1) : ;;
*) die2 "$DOC has $count \`\`\`yaml fences and this gate mirrors exactly one — it cannot tell which is the copy of $WORKFLOW" ;;
esac

open_line=$opens
body_start=$((open_line + 1))

# The closing fence is the first bare ``` at or after the first body line. `sed` prints the tail and
# `grep -n` numbers it from there, so the offset is relative and has to be added back.
tail_lines=$(sed -n "${body_start},\$p" "$DOC")
close_off=$(printf '%s\n' "$tail_lines" | grep -n '^```$' | head -1 | cut -d: -f1) || close_off=""
[ -n "$close_off" ] || die2 "$DOC:$open_line opens a \`\`\`yaml fence that is never closed"

close_line=$((body_start + close_off - 1))
body_end=$((close_line - 1))

[ "$body_end" -ge "$body_start" ] || die2 "$DOC:$open_line opens a \`\`\`yaml fence with no content"

block=$(sed -n "${body_start},${body_end}p" "$DOC")
[ -n "$block" ] || die2 "$DOC:$open_line opens a \`\`\`yaml fence with no content"

# `diff` rather than a string compare, so a finding says WHAT differs. The labels carry the real
# paths — including the doc's line range, because the block has no filename of its own and a reader
# otherwise has to go and find it.
if printf '%s\n' "$block" | diff -u \
	--label "$WORKFLOW" "$WORKFLOW" \
	--label "$DOC:${body_start}-${body_end} (the \`\`\`yaml block)" -; then
	printf '%s: clean — %s:%s-%s is byte-identical to %s\n' "$ME" "$DOC" "$body_start" "$body_end" "$WORKFLOW"
	exit 0
fi

cat >&2 <<EOF

$ME: THE MIRRORED BLOCK AND THE WORKFLOW HAVE DRIFTED
    $WORKFLOW
    $DOC:${body_start}-${body_end}

Both are printed above and NEITHER is authoritative — this gate does not know which side is
right, deliberately. quince#618 is why: the doc was correct and the workflow was wrong, so a gate
that resolved toward the running file would have picked the wrong side on the only instance there
has been. Decide, then make them identical again.

If the workflow is the side that must change, this seat can make that edit: quince-coder holds
workflows: write since 2026-08-17 (quince#1116). Do not route it to the Operator.
EOF
exit 1
